        "b_sci":  "Hypophthalmichthys nobilis",
        "b_first":  "Native to big rivers of eastern Asia, bighead carp came to U.S. fish farms in 1973 to help clean ponds. Fish escaped and spread through the Mississippi, Missouri, and Illinois basins.",
        "b_path":  "With an adult, report a sighting to USGS NAS with a photo, place, and date. If you catch one, never release it or move it to another location. Freeze it and tell your state wildlife agency.",
        "b_impacts":  "A bighead carp has no true stomach, so it filters tiny plankton almost nonstop. Native paddlefish, buffalo, and shad need that same food, and young fish do too.",
        "b_detect":  "Carp leave eDNA, tiny bits of DNA shed into the water, and USGS tests river samples for it. USGS also tags carp with transmitters, and in some places underwater receivers alert managers when one swims past.",
        "b_manage":  "Army Corps and state teams use electric, sound, and CO2 barriers and targeted harvest. USGS gives them carp movement data and population models so they can place deterrents and set removal goals.",
