        "b_first":  "Two forms invaded: one from India and Sri Lanka in the South, one from Korea in the North. Aquarium dumping started it in 1950s Florida, but it is not just a Florida problem.",
        "b_path":  "With an adult, report hydrilla to USGS NAS with photos and the spot. Pull every plant scrap off boats, then drain and dry them, because one stem piece can start a new patch.",
        "b_impacts":  "Hydrilla can grow up to an inch a day and fill a lake from bottom to top. Boats get stuck, native plants lose light, and fish can run short of oxygen.",
        "b_detect":  "Look for rings of 4 to 8 toothed leaves. The USGS NAS database of non-native water species maps checked sightings and emails alerts when hydrilla turns up somewhere new.",
        "b_manage":  "State managers and the Army Corps use herbicides and permitted sterile grass carp. USGS gives them maps and alerts to catch new patches early. USGS scientists also helped explain eagle deaths linked to hydrilla.",
