        "b_sci":  "Bromus tectorum",
        "b_first":  "Native to Europe, North Africa, and southwest Asia, cheatgrass hid in contaminated seed and straw in the mid-to-late 1800s. Overgrazed land gave it room. Its biggest infestations are now in the West.",
        "b_path":  "Brush barbed seeds off socks, pets, and gear. Avoid parking on dry grass; hot exhaust can start a fire. Found a new patch in healthy sagebrush? With an adult, report it to EDDMapS.",
        "b_impacts":  "Cheatgrass sprouts early and cheats native plants of water, then dries to kindling by summer. It helps fuel a cycle of bigger wildfires that wipe out sagebrush and wildlife habitat.",
        "b_detect":  "Cheatgrass turns red-tan early, so satellites and ground surveys can spot it. USGS used over 43,000 plant plots to map where it is likely to take over, so managers know where to act first.",
        "b_manage":  "BLM, USFWS, and other land managers use herbicide, targeted grazing, and native replanting. USGS field tests show which treatments last, and suggest protecting healthy sagebrush beats repairing burned land.",
