        "b_sci":  "Oryctes rhinoceros",
        "b_first":  "Native to Southeast Asia, it spread across the Pacific in the early to mid 1900s. It reached Guam in 2007 and Oahu in 2013, and its grubs ride in mulch, compost, and potted plants.",
        "b_path":  "Saw V-shaped cuts in a palm? Photograph it from a distance. On neighbor islands, an adult can call or text 643-PEST (808-643-7378). Never move mulch or green waste, because grubs live in it.",
        "b_impacts":  "The coconut palm is the \"tree of life\" on many Pacific islands. A horned, two-inch beetle bores into its crown, eats new fronds, and can kill the palm.",
        "b_detect":  "Teams look for V-shaped frond cuts, hang pheromone (scent-lure) traps, and use dogs to sniff out breeding piles. USGS uses trap records to map where Hawaii\u0027s climate suits the beetle.",
        "b_manage":  "USDA APHIS and Hawaii\u0027s agriculture department clear breeding piles, treat palms, and enforce quarantines. USGS adds a climate map that shows managers where the beetle could spread next, including native forests.",
