        "b_common":  "Golden Mussel",
        "b_sci":  "Limnoperna fortunei",
        "b_first":  "Native to China and Southeast Asia, it likely rode in ship ballast water (water carried to balance ships). Found in Stockton, California in October 2024, it is new to North America and spreading.",
        "b_path":  "See mussels on a dock, hull, or pipe? Photograph them beside a coin and report them at USGS NAS \"Report a Sighting,\" with an adult. Every golden mussel report triggers an alert. Boaters: clean, drain, and dry everything.",
        "b_impacts":  "Colonies elsewhere have packed in up to 250,000 mussels per square meter, clogging pipes that carry drinking and farm water. They also crowd out native mussels and strip fish food.",
        "b_detect":  "California crews use tow nets for larvae (baby-stage mussels), inspect docks, and test water for eDNA (DNA organisms leave behind). USGS\u0027s Nonindigenous Aquatic Species (NAS) database maps each verified report.",
        "b_manage":  "California agencies run the response and boat checks. USGS gives them and nearby states an alert for every golden mussel report, so neighbors can prepare. In big waters, removal often fails.",
