        "b_common":  "Black Carp",
        "b_sci":  "Mylopharyngodon piceus",
        "b_first":  "Native to eastern Asia, black carp arrived unnoticed, mixed into imported grass carp. Farms used them to eat snails that host fish parasites. Escapes in the 1990s reached the Mississippi basin.",
        "b_path":  "Think you\u0027ve found a black carp? Photograph it safely and report it at USGS NAS \"Report a Sighting,\" with an adult. Anglers: keep a catch on ice and call your state about the bounty.",
        "b_impacts":  "Black carp can top 100 pounds. Molar-like throat teeth (pharyngeal teeth) crush mussels and snails, many already rare. They also eat insects native fish need.",
        "b_detect":  "Commercial catches and nets turn up black carp, and eDNA (DNA organisms leave behind) can flag where to look. USGS tests whether they can reproduce. Triploid fish (three sets of chromosomes) cannot reproduce, but most wild black carp are fertile.",
        "b_manage":  "States run a bounty on black carp, and federal law bans importing live ones. USGS gives them fertility tests and biology findings so they can target control. Big rivers have no proven fix.",
        "b_qr":  "https://nas.er.usgs.gov/queries/factsheet.aspx?SpeciesID=573",
